timately what freaked me out  the most but also what excited me the  most about this whole thing was just  that  we are doing something familiar  you know people want to call it a reboot  but it is  there's some key differences there and i  think that that is what people have  really latched onto and why it's been  successful and why people have enjoyed  it is because it's really not the same  thing  it's a whole different voice and you  know grant some people probably don't  like it for that reason but sure we we  had to show progression for that reason  yeah i love that answer and yeah i'll  never call it a reboot again i'm sorry i  even said i'm sorry  i don't want it to feel like i'm calling  you out because that's what everybody  calls it it's you and everybody else  no i love it i i feel like i've learned  this is great and i i'm wondering too  you i think you just mentioned it too  that so you would say that this revival  is more of  is it  also geared more towards an older  audience like 10 people who are doubting  like oh am i too old to watch this like  can we enjoy it as well yeah the whole  point is there is now no one too old to  watch it and to be fair that was our  desire with the original show too we  wanted to have a show that  ages 5 to 95 could sit down and still  laugh at the same jokes together in the  same gag so  in this way in in a sense the the  content is probably not as kid friendly  so it definitely is geared more toward  the um maybe 15 to 95  right age range um so uh we it we have  in a sense narrowed ourselves a little  bit but i think the whole point now is  that we're very much opening ourselves  up toward adult situations adult  problems uh relatable things that the  millennial audience can appreciate and  to be fair you know it's funny because  sometimes i feel like i'm aged out of  the show because there are so many jokes  that i did not understand at all  when we were making it and i was just  going guys i don't know if this is that  funny but then it would come out and  they were the ones that would go totally  viral they were the ones that people  were laughing at the most and then i  just realized oh maybe i'm just an old  fart  you know like  i'm not even 30 yet but there's already  things that i'm not cool enough to  understand so there are definitely  references and jokes that the  even older than me side of the audience  might not necessarily understand it it's  certainly  niche in the late teens to 20s but um  but again the the goal is that everybody  can have something to laugh at on this  show yeah and i love that and i like you  mentioned the niche thing and it's doing  something different i think which  everyone can appreciate and  do you think too it's it's so cool to  see that like you have a trajectory  where you were a teen when you were on  it and it's first run and now you're  older is it like do you find things more  enjoyable now doing it as you're older  on set with the same cast or is it like  different like what would you kind of  say is maybe comparison  yeah i think um  in us in a sense  some things are easier like i'm not in  school  when i'm trying to do this thing so i'm  not i'm not only trying to  shoot the finale but then also take my  final right an hour later so there's  there was a lot of split focus there  where i was really trying to focus on  academics at the same time as work and  that's something that i don't have to  worry about anymore so but i've traded  it off now for  um at least in this season uh producing  the show and also directing several  episodes of the show so my mental  bandwidth is now focused on not just  what i'm saying and doing as an actor  and and what i'm relating to with  whoever i'm having dialogue with it's  also thinking about pre-production for  the next episode um post-production for  the previous episode so there's still a  lot of things pulling focus but that  is also what has made it so fun is  really feeling like  we as  as the original characters have a a  strong say  in what makes it to screen you know if  there's something that we feel like  isn't authentic to the character or  something that we feel like wouldn't fit  in the universe  uh we're we're very lucky to have a  writing team that's very receptive and  open to that and they really want to  make this a team effort and work  together on it so  um  you know sometimes that can be tricky  when people have different creative  opinions but that's where the dialogue  starts and um and we've gotten i think  to a point where  we're all getting  better and better every week and every  season at  starting to speak the same language and  get on the same page faster and faster  and i think that that is another thing  that has moved it along pretty darn  smoothly  in this sort of adult version because  there could be there's a lot of  opportunity for conflict there because  like i said there is no road map no one  knows exactly what to do  and you get a lot of talented creative  people in a room there's going to be  different ideas floating around  so that was one of the biggest  challenges but also i think one of the  biggest triumphs of the show was  distilling down the best ideas from all  sides and using it to sort of  create this thing under this lens of  the general question what would the fans  want  right um and i think that was our our  ultimate goal and our our top priority  was taking all of our ideas but  filtering it through  what is our audience looking for  sure and i think it's really cool that  you direct and produce on it now and  they did an episode last season and i  wonder too you know do you feel like  from acting at a young age you can  direct now younger actors better on set  do you feel like there's things you  would only know from that experience  that you can kind of almost be a mentor  in a sense definitely that's something  that i've leveraged really from the  beginning we finished icarly in 2012 and  i started directing another nickelodeon  series henry danger in 2014. so pretty  shortly after our run ended i was doing  that and then another nickelodeon show  uh game shakers and it was all you know  very young kids there were adults um of  course in the cast but predominantly it  was these kids who had done very little  work in the past and this was their  first kind of big break you know so i  was definitely able to put myself in  their shoes and and multi-camera  especially is a very difficult medium  because it's this combination of tv and  theater  where you can't move and talk in a  normal way as if you were just walking  around a room in a 360 degree set you  have to make sure that when you're  turning around you have to turn around  in the right direction you know you have  to look over your shoulder toward camera  not away from camera even if it feels  unnatural so there is little there's  these little tips and tricks and things  that i was trying to instill in them  early on with their sort of stage craft  that that i learned you know and that i  was able to sort of leverage myself and  and kind of see those problems  before they happened right and and just  start getting those little things into  their heads and and even just from the  performance side um just knowing from my  from my practice in talking with kids on  those kids shows being able to leverage  that into when we have guest cast that  are kids or talking with jaden who has  become you know  very very very close to a true fake  daughter  right  over over these last couple years i'm  able to talk to her in a way that i know  that i would have understood  back in the day yeah and i think it's  interesting you talking about that  different stage style directing too and  i'm wondering do you plan on do you see  yourself then branching out and doing  more regular type tv directing and film  directing is that something you want to  branch out to yeah you know  multi-camera sitcoms are a warm fuzzy  blanket for me i grew up on them they're  comfortable they are really fun  uh from from the purely logistical  family perspective they're the best  schedule ever right right it's really  hard having a family when you're doing  night shoots on a cw drama  right down down in san pedro you know  and you're never seeing your children so  um i love multicam so much and i  definitely want to use the fact that  icarly is an adult show to leverage that  into me doing other adult shows i know  it's a little bit uncommon for someone  my age to be directing you know these  titans of comedy um and so that's  already something that i'm gonna have to  work through but uh but that is  absolutely something that i want to do  and then ultimately expand those  mindsets and skills into doing the  single camera stuff like you're saying  like single camera tv and features and  everything else so it's all part of the  process  no yeah and i'm excited it's gonna  happen i'm excited to see what you bring  to the table with that too and it's cool  you mentioned family and you know  being a dad now and you have a second  daughter congrats recently thank you um  it's awesome that you do this great  podcast with brett davern radioactive  dad so  do you love doing just podcasting in  general as much as you do the acting and  directing and is this something you  could also see you doing more podcasts  or opening more to that is that  something you really seriously want to  commit time to yeah you know i i think  of all the things podcasting i  it's hard to even consider myself a  podcaster because the show that we do  is essentially just  a candid ad lib conversation that i  liked having with brett every week just  as a friend and as a dad and a fellow  actor and the whole concept of the show  came about when we just sort of realized  you know  if we just did what we're doing right  now sitting at this diner but there was  a microphone right here  this would essentially be a podcast yeah  with with a little bit more structure to  it and granted we tried to do structure  for a while and you know ultimately that  just didn't really we liked it better  just meandering on in our own thoughts  and getting off on tangents aside from  the fact that we like taking you know  user questions uh as kind of our final  segment and dipping our hands into the  mail bag and that's been one of the  super fun things is you know in the same  way that we have grown up with the  icarly generation that watched the show  those people are becoming parents now  and they're starting to have the same  issues that we're going through as  parents you know and and they're asking  questions and we're able to kind of give  advice and speak from our own  experiences and for me that that show is  just catharsis i just like sitting down  with a friend and talking about the  stuff that i'm going through and 90 of  the time it's an issue that he's already  dealt with his kids are a couple years  older than me so he's a little bit of my  dad mentor in a way sure he's he's  worked through some of this stuff so um  it's just nice because  guys don't usually like talking to each  other especially about their problems  so um having  a safe person to to talk with this stuff  about and not only that but we're  recording it for other people to hear so  they get to hear my problems and there's  a very likely chance that they're  dealing with some of them too so um it's  kind of just become this  collaborative therapy time that we all  get to do together and and i hope that  dads especially young dads who are going  through a lot of the stuff with the  young kids just have a  safe place that they can go to  work through our episodes and feel like  they're learning a little something or  they're at the very least not going  through what they're going through alone  yeah and that's awesome that you do that  and i like that you're saying it gives  you catharsis and that it's coming from  a place that's like 